{"id": "unknown-2026-10-16-017", "date": "2026-10-16", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "The Hydrogen Stream: P2X starts selling renewable methane to European markets", "content": "<p>Europe’s renewable hydrogen and e-fuels sector continues to gain momentum, with new developments spanning e-methane certification and offtake, electrolyser technology, large-scale green hydrogen projects and strategic investments. Recent announcements from companies including P2X Solutions, Stargate Hydrogen, ITM Power, Nel and Hy24</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/the-hydrogen-stream-p2x-starts-selling-renewable-methane-to-european-markets/\">The Hydrogen Stream", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/the-hydrogen-stream-p2x-starts-selling-renewable-methane-to-european-markets/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "trina-2026-10-16-018", "date": "2026-10-16", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": ["trina"], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "positive", "score": 0.5, "keywords": ["expand", "improve"]}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "Chinese PV Industry Brief: Trina Solar expands upstream ties with stake in encapsulation film maker", "content": "<p>Trina Solar plans to acquire a 1.6% stake in Chinese PV encapsulation film manufacturer Betterial for CNY 40 million ($5.88 million). The investment aims to strengthen upstream supplier ties and improve supply chain stability.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/chinese-pv-industry-brief-trina-solar-expands-upstream-ties-with-stake-in-encapsulation-film-maker/\">Chinese PV Industry Brief: Trina Solar expands upstream ties with stake in encapsulation film maker</a> a", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/chinese-pv-industry-brief-trina-solar-expands-upstream-ties-with-stake-in-encapsulation-film-maker/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-10-16-019", "date": "2026-10-16", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "positive", "score": 0.75, "keywords": ["record", "increase"]}, "importance": {"score": 0.65, "reasons": ["情緒極端"]}, "title": "Big highs and lows across Asia in September", "content": "<p>In a new weekly update for <b>pv magazine</b>, Solcast, a DNV company, reports that September 2026 saw extreme solar irradiance variations across Asia, with Japan and Borneo experiencing up to 30% below-normal levels, while China, Korea, the Philippines and eastern Indonesia recorded significant surpluses. Typhoons drove much of the variability, while strengthening El Niño is expected to bring sunnier conditions to Southeast Asia and increased cloudiness to Japan and eastern China through yea", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/big-highs-and-lows-across-asia-in-september/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-10-16-020", "date": "2026-10-16", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["grid_parity", "industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "positive", "score": 0.67, "keywords": ["leading", "growth", "increase"]}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "Electrifying everything", "content": "<p>COP31 aims to increase electricity’s share of global final energy demand from 20% to 35% by 2035, accelerating the transition to clean, affordable energy through electrification. Australia is leading with rapid growth in solar, wind, batteries and EVs, demonstrating how renewable energy can lower electricity costs, reduce fossil fuel dependence and support decarbonisation.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/electrifying-everything/\">Electrifying everything</a> app", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/electrifying-everything/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-10-16-021", "date": "2026-10-16", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "China could produce the cheapest green hydrogen", "content": "<p>China could produce green hydrogen at an average cost of $120/MWh, significantly less than Germany, the United States and Japan, according to researchers at the University of Cologne. The analysis highlights China's cost advantages and the supply security risks associated with hydrogen imports.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/china-can-produce-the-cheapest-green-hydrogen/\">China could produce the cheapest green hydrogen</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://ww", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/china-can-produce-the-cheapest-green-hydrogen/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-10-16-022", "date": "2026-10-16", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q4", "month": 10, "week": 42, "weekday": "Fri"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "Germany recommends eliminating direct internet access to PV plant control systems", "content": "<p>Germany’s Federal Office for Information Security (BSI) recommends eliminating direct internet access to PV plant control systems to reduce cybersecurity risks. It also calls for secure remote connections, multifactor authentication, regular software updates and periodic security checks.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/germany-recommends-eliminating-direct-internet-access-to-pv-plant-control-systems/\">Germany recommends eliminating direct internet access to PV ", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/16/germany-recommends-eliminating-direct-internet-access-to-pv-plant-control-systems/", "type": "rss"}]}
